-- lib/work.cub — the record of the work on a repository, as data of the language (2026-11-12).
-- Loaded after lib/prelude.cub (List). Nothing here is specific to this repository: the goals of a
-- particular piece of work are a data type that work declares (this repository's: work/goals.cub).
--
-- t64 (2026-11-12): needs-work (goal work-object). This record is to be rebuilt on the work object
-- (work/goals.cub, work-object): tickets and goals are open loops, opened and closed by anyone, with
-- no kind enum (the user judged wrong / maybe-wrong / needs-work / question weak: what matters is why
-- a loop was opened and which root goal it serves) and no author. Facts the code determines (what
-- loads, what is inhabited, what was refused and why) are never tickets: they are computed (t61,
-- t62). Until then this record is used as it is, from 2026-11-12.
--
-- A goal is a constructor of that data type, and the work's `serves : G → List G` lists the goals
-- each is instrumental to (a goal may serve several). A ticket is a checked definition
-- `tN : Ticket G`: its kind, the goals it serves, and whether it is open (`opened`; `open` is a
-- reserved word) or closed on a date (YYYYMMDD as a word). The ticket's text is not in the record. It is the one comment that begins
-- `tN (YYYY-MM-DD):`, written where in the code the ticket's subject is (any file, any comment
-- syntax), so a reader of that place sees it without knowing the ticket exists. tools/work.py lists,
-- shows and checks tickets: every record has exactly one such comment and every such comment a
-- record. Opening, closing and editing a ticket are changes to the code; the repository's history is
-- the succession of these states.
--
-- Kinds: wrong (definitely incorrect, and why), maybe-wrong (possibly incorrect, and why), needs-work
-- (a specific piece of work or review is owed here), question (something not yet known).
-- After (2026-11-12): `tN-after : List (Ticket G)` names the tickets that must be closed before tN
-- can be: closing them closes tN or is what tN waits on. A reference by definition, as any relation
-- here is; written after every record it names. A ticket with no such definition waits on nothing.
-- Value and priority (2026-11-12). Priority is not stored: it is computed from what the record states
-- about how value depends on work (tools/work.py next, until it is a term, t37). The work states:
--   serves g        what g is for. A goal that serves nothing is a root, and is declared one (roots);
--                   a goal serving nothing that is not declared a root is an error, as is a ticket
--                   serving no goal. Roots render at one level, as the top priorities.
--   value g         how g's value depends on what serves it: all-of (nothing until every part is
--                   complete: a companion repository with half a core is not one) or each-of (every
--                   completed part delivers on its own).
--   tN-after        what must be complete before tN can be done (below).
--   speeds g        the goals whose work g makes faster when g's parts land (the knowledge system
--                   speeds all work), and tN-speeds likewise for a ticket.
-- From these: a piece of work's value lands when it and everything its value is gated on are
-- complete. Work whose value is gated on incomplete work comes after that work even when nothing
-- stops it being started; among work for one all-of goal, what speeds other work comes first; the
-- total time is the same in any order, so the order only moves when value lands.
data Combine = all-of | each-of
data Kind = wrong | maybe-wrong | needs-work | question
data Status = opened | closed (on : Word)
record Ticket (G : Type) = { t-kind : Kind ; t-goals : List G ; t-status : Status }
ticket : {G : Type} → Kind → List G → Status → Ticket G
ticket {G} k gs s = Ticket { t-kind = k ; t-goals = gs ; t-status = s }
is-open : {G : Type} → Ticket G → Bool
is-open {G} t = case t-status {G} t of opened → true; closed d → false
